1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,708
When a page needs a file,

2
00:00:02,708 --> 00:00:05,400
the browser checks its own cache first.

3
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:08,108
One question decides what happens next.

4
00:00:08,108 --> 00:00:10,415
Do I have a fresh copy?

5
00:00:10,815 --> 00:00:14,892
Let's follow one file, style.css, through three requests.

6
00:00:15,292 --> 00:00:16,062
First visit.

7
00:00:16,062 --> 00:00:17,600
The cache is empty,

8
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:19,908
so this is a cache miss.

9
00:00:20,308 --> 00:00:23,769
So the browser sends GET /style.css to the server.

10
00:00:24,169 --> 00:00:26,092
The server replies 200 OK

11
00:00:26,092 --> 00:00:29,569
with the whole file and two caching headers.

12
00:00:29,969 --> 00:00:32,662
Cache-Control: max-age=3600 says the copy stays fresh

13
00:00:32,662 --> 00:00:33,815
for 3600 seconds.

14
00:00:33,815 --> 00:00:35,569
That's one hour.

15
00:00:35,969 --> 00:00:38,277
The ETag is a version tag

16
00:00:38,277 --> 00:00:40,969
for this exact file, like a fingerprint.

17
00:00:41,369 --> 00:00:43,292
The browser uses the file

18
00:00:43,292 --> 00:00:45,985
and saves a copy, headers and all.

19
00:00:45,985 --> 00:00:48,308
Its age starts at zero.

20
00:00:48,708 --> 00:00:51,015
DevTools logs it as status 200,

21
00:00:51,015 --> 00:00:52,554
with the full size.

22
00:00:52,954 --> 00:00:55,646
Ten minutes later, you open another page.

23
00:00:55,646 --> 00:00:57,185
It needs style.css too.

24
00:00:57,585 --> 00:00:59,892
The copy is 600 seconds old.

25
00:00:59,892 --> 00:01:02,985
That's under 3600, so it's still fresh.

26
00:01:03,385 --> 00:01:06,462
So the browser uses its copy right away.

27
00:01:06,462 --> 00:01:08,385
Nothing goes over the network.

28
00:01:08,785 --> 00:01:10,323
That's a cache hit.

29
00:01:10,323 --> 00:01:13,015
No network wait, and no data used.

30
00:01:13,415 --> 00:01:14,954
DevTools still lists it,

31
00:01:14,954 --> 00:01:17,646
but the Size column says disk cache

32
00:01:17,646 --> 00:01:19,400
or memory cache.

33
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:21,338
Later, you come back.

34
00:01:21,338 --> 00:01:24,031
The copy is now two hours old.

35
00:01:24,431 --> 00:01:27,508
That's past max-age, so the copy is stale.

36
00:01:27,508 --> 00:01:30,215
But stale does not mean deleted.

37
00:01:30,615 --> 00:01:33,308
So the browser checks with the server.

38
00:01:33,308 --> 00:01:36,769
It sends the stored ETag in an If-None-Match header.

39
00:01:37,169 --> 00:01:38,323
In plain words:

40
00:01:38,323 --> 00:01:41,015
has the file changed since version a3f9?

41
00:01:41,415 --> 00:01:43,338
The server's tag still matches.

42
00:01:43,338 --> 00:01:45,646
So it replies 304 Not Modified.

43
00:01:46,046 --> 00:01:47,969
A 304 has no body.

44
00:01:47,969 --> 00:01:50,877
It's only headers, so it's tiny.

45
00:01:51,277 --> 00:01:54,738
The browser reuses its copy and updates its headers.

46
00:01:54,738 --> 00:01:57,062
It's fresh for another hour.

47
00:01:57,462 --> 00:02:00,538
DevTools shows status 304 and a small size.

48
00:02:00,938 --> 00:02:04,400
If the file had changed, the tags would differ.

49
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:06,323
Then the server sends 200

50
00:02:06,323 --> 00:02:10,200
with the new file and a new ETag.

51
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:11,400
Three requests.

52
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:13,800
Three outcomes.

53
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:16,508
Your Cache-Control header picks the path.

54
00:02:16,508 --> 00:02:18,046
max-age=3600 means path two

55
00:02:18,046 --> 00:02:20,754
for an hour, then path three.

56
00:02:21,154 --> 00:02:23,462
With no-cache, the copy is stored

57
00:02:23,462 --> 00:02:25,385
but checked first, every time.

58
00:02:25,385 --> 00:02:26,538
That's path three.

59
00:02:26,938 --> 00:02:28,862
With no-store, nothing is saved.

60
00:02:28,862 --> 00:02:31,169
Every request is a first visit.

61
00:02:31,569 --> 00:02:33,877
To replay a first visit yourself,

62
00:02:33,877 --> 00:02:35,800
tick Disable cache in DevTools.

63
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:38,508
A fresh hit uses no network.

64
00:02:38,508 --> 00:02:41,200
A 304 costs one small round trip.

65
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:45,508
A 200 sends the whole file.
